WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.267 --> 00:00:03.335
-Our first guest tonight
is an Emmy Award-winning

00:00:03.335 --> 00:00:05.271
comedian and actor
you know from shows such as

00:00:05.271 --> 00:00:07.173
"The Office," "Extras,"
and "After Life."

00:00:07.173 --> 00:00:09.475
His latest comedy special,
"Supernature,"

00:00:09.475 --> 00:00:10.710
begins streaming
on Netflix May 24th.

00:00:10.710 --> 00:00:12.211
Let's take a look.

00:00:12.211 --> 00:00:14.613
-People quiz me on Twitter when
they find out I'm an atheist.

00:00:14.613 --> 00:00:16.749
They go, "Well, you don't
believe in God at all?"

00:00:16.749 --> 00:00:18.952
I go, "No."
"Do you pray?"

00:00:18.952 --> 00:00:21.120
I go, "No."

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:23.889
They go, "Why don't you pray
just in case there's a God?"

00:00:23.889 --> 00:00:26.259
And I say, "Why don't you put
garlic over your door

00:00:26.259 --> 00:00:28.194
just in case
there's a Dracula," right?

00:00:28.194 --> 00:00:30.697
I've got no problem
with praying, you know?

00:00:30.697 --> 00:00:33.699
I know loads of nice Christians
and Muslims and Jews.

00:00:33.699 --> 00:00:35.668
And if one of my family is
very ill, they always say,

00:00:35.668 --> 00:00:36.769
"Oh, I'll pray for them."

00:00:36.769 --> 00:00:37.971
And I always say,
"Oh, thanks very much,"

00:00:37.971 --> 00:00:39.339
'cause it's a nice gesture.

00:00:39.339 --> 00:00:41.674
If they said, "Oh, we also
canceled the chemotherapy,"

00:00:41.674 --> 00:00:42.675
I go, "Don't do that.

00:00:42.675 --> 00:00:44.877
Don't do that."

00:00:44.877 --> 00:00:48.381
Try -- Fill your boots, son,
but let's --

00:00:48.381 --> 00:00:51.284
Let's do the praying and
the chemotherapy, shall we?

00:00:51.284 --> 00:00:54.854
'Cause that's the same result as
just the chemotherapy.

00:00:54.854 --> 00:00:59.559
So let's definitely keep
that one, shall we?

00:00:59.559 --> 00:01:02.962
-Please welcome back to the show
our good friend Ricky Gervais.

00:01:02.962 --> 00:01:05.565
[ Cheers and applause ]
♪♪

00:01:17.977 --> 00:01:21.247
Welcome back, Ricky.
-Oh!

00:01:21.247 --> 00:01:23.816
-It's always lovely
to have you here in person,

00:01:23.816 --> 00:01:25.551
though you did seem to enjoy --

00:01:25.551 --> 00:01:26.852
We spoke
a few times via Zoom,

00:01:26.852 --> 00:01:29.021
and I always thought
it was such a coincidence that

00:01:29.021 --> 00:01:33.125
you found your way right in
front of all your awards.

00:01:33.125 --> 00:01:34.260
-They're not all of them.

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:37.230
[ Laughter ]

00:01:37.230 --> 00:01:39.298
Well, I didn't set them up.

00:01:39.298 --> 00:01:42.101
I just -- that was my --
-Oh, I don't know.

00:01:42.101 --> 00:01:44.304
These Emmys look very set up.

00:01:44.304 --> 00:01:46.205
I mean, they're
very complimentary.

00:01:46.205 --> 00:01:48.808
-That's not even all the BAFTAs.
There's three more there.

00:01:48.808 --> 00:01:50.576
-So seven BAFTAs.
Did you ever win a Golden Globe?

00:01:50.576 --> 00:01:53.446
I mean, obviously,
you hosted it to great regard.

00:01:53.446 --> 00:01:54.547
-I won two.

00:01:54.547 --> 00:01:56.548
They're over this side
of the room.

00:01:56.548 --> 00:01:57.783
-So you can see them.

00:01:57.783 --> 00:02:00.153
When you Zoom with me,
you can see the Globes.

00:02:00.153 --> 00:02:03.656
So, we talked a little backstage
about how, you know,

00:02:03.656 --> 00:02:05.257
the pandemic, in a way,
allowed us to be

00:02:05.257 --> 00:02:07.059
a little lazier with ourselves.

00:02:07.059 --> 00:02:08.661
Did you take to the laziness?

00:02:08.661 --> 00:02:11.831
-Yeah, it suited me
just being at home,

00:02:11.831 --> 00:02:13.066
no one coming to the house.

00:02:13.066 --> 00:02:15.534
I didn't even have to lie
to get out of stuff.

00:02:15.534 --> 00:02:16.669
-Yeah.
-Do you know what I mean?

00:02:16.669 --> 00:02:18.537
Like Christenings
and funerals and weddings.

00:02:18.537 --> 00:02:19.538
They were all canceled.
-Yeah.

00:02:19.538 --> 00:02:20.840
-So it was like -- I was like,

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:23.776
"Oh, and I was looking forward
to that," you know.

00:02:23.776 --> 00:02:25.245
But it has ruined me.
Now I don't want to do anything.

00:02:25.245 --> 00:02:26.279
-Yeah.

00:02:26.279 --> 00:02:27.613
-I don't want do
anything ever again.

00:02:27.613 --> 00:02:30.049
-But that's why I'm so impressed
that you not only have

00:02:30.049 --> 00:02:32.018
this special,
you're out on the road again.

00:02:32.018 --> 00:02:35.421
But "Supernature" --
why did you name it this?

00:02:35.421 --> 00:02:39.092
-The title comes from
two reasons, really.

00:02:39.092 --> 00:02:41.560
One, I sort of debunk
the supernatural.

00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:43.328
I don't believe in
anything supernatural.

00:02:43.328 --> 00:02:45.164
So I go through all that.

00:02:45.164 --> 00:02:49.101
And, also, I say that nature is
super enough, you know?

00:02:49.101 --> 00:02:50.403
I mean, we don't need --

00:02:50.403 --> 00:02:53.806
I don't need unicorns
and fairies and that.

00:02:53.806 --> 00:02:55.741
And I say, we've got
the octopus, you know,

00:02:55.741 --> 00:02:56.942
things like that,

00:02:56.942 --> 00:03:00.112
and the duckbill platypus
is a monotreme, right?

00:03:00.112 --> 00:03:03.649
When people first discovered
the duckbill platypus,

00:03:03.649 --> 00:03:05.551
they thought
it was a hoax, right?

00:03:05.551 --> 00:03:09.522
They did. And that's true.
And it produces eggs and milk.

00:03:09.522 --> 00:03:11.557
It could make its own custard.
-Yeah.

00:03:11.557 --> 00:03:14.593
-It doesn't, but it could.

00:03:14.593 --> 00:03:15.928
-It could.

00:03:15.928 --> 00:03:19.899
-So, I go into why love nature
and why I hate people.

00:03:19.899 --> 00:03:23.068
That's the --
that's the main theme. Yeah.

00:03:23.068 --> 00:03:25.771
-You've always been not just
an atheist,

00:03:25.771 --> 00:03:27.306
but sort of proudly an atheist.

00:03:27.306 --> 00:03:30.309
That clip is
a wonderful example of that.

00:03:30.309 --> 00:03:34.213
And as the clip sort of details,
people continue to

00:03:34.213 --> 00:03:35.814
come at you for this
and think that

00:03:35.814 --> 00:03:37.583
you're on the wrong side
of this position.

00:03:37.583 --> 00:03:39.819
-Well, I mean, Twitter is --

00:03:39.819 --> 00:03:42.255
I mean, yeah, there's
a billion people on Twitter.

00:03:42.255 --> 00:03:45.758
And, you know, it's like
looking at every toilet wall

00:03:45.758 --> 00:03:47.627
in the world at once, isn't it?

00:03:47.627 --> 00:03:48.628
You mustn't take it serious.

00:03:48.628 --> 00:03:50.129
So with all those
people on there,

00:03:50.129 --> 00:03:52.064
you know someone's
gonna say something stupid.

00:03:52.064 --> 00:03:53.199
I mean, I look for them.

00:03:53.199 --> 00:03:55.435
I mean, I like --

00:03:55.435 --> 00:03:57.703
That's the good thing about
Twitter, I guess.

00:03:57.703 --> 00:04:00.306
Like 20 years ago,
an observational comic

00:04:00.306 --> 00:04:02.742
would actually
have to go to, like,

00:04:02.742 --> 00:04:05.411
homes and prisons to
meet the people

00:04:05.411 --> 00:04:07.580
that talk to you on Twitter.

00:04:07.580 --> 00:04:10.315
So I can do it from the safety
of my Hampstead mansion.

00:04:10.315 --> 00:04:14.520
I can speak to the worst people

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:17.790
in the world
and formulate comedy.

00:04:17.790 --> 00:04:19.792
-So, yeah --
So your observational comedy

00:04:19.792 --> 00:04:21.794
comes from a place of great
comfort for you.

00:04:21.794 --> 00:04:23.262
You've managed to
observe through your phone.

00:04:23.262 --> 00:04:24.496
-Exactly. Yeah, exactly.
-Yeah.

00:04:24.496 --> 00:04:28.100
-I can talk to the
maddest people on the Earth.

00:04:28.100 --> 00:04:30.469
-You talk about health.

00:04:30.469 --> 00:04:32.071
We were talking backstage.

00:04:32.071 --> 00:04:34.507
For all intents and purposes,
you feel like people our age

00:04:34.507 --> 00:04:35.808
are living on borrowed time.

00:04:35.808 --> 00:04:38.978
-I think we're not meant to
live this long.

00:04:38.978 --> 00:04:41.614
-Yeah.
-I'm 60. It's not natural.

00:04:41.614 --> 00:04:45.484
When we were hunter-gatherers,
we'd make it to about --

00:04:45.484 --> 00:04:47.319
I don't know --
25, if we were lucky.

00:04:47.319 --> 00:04:50.056
Then we'd get a scratch -- dead.
You know what I mean?

00:04:50.056 --> 00:04:51.557
But now we're just kept alive.

00:04:51.557 --> 00:04:53.092
They replace everything.

00:04:53.092 --> 00:04:56.395
You know, they can't do the
head transplant yet,

00:04:56.395 --> 00:04:57.529
but that, you know --

00:04:57.529 --> 00:04:58.798
-Would you --
-I would, yeah.

00:04:58.798 --> 00:05:00.833
Because you are --
I only care about that.

00:05:00.833 --> 00:05:02.301
I don't really care about
this rubbish.

00:05:02.301 --> 00:05:03.402
-Okay.

00:05:03.402 --> 00:05:05.571
-This just gets me around
to do stuff.

00:05:05.571 --> 00:05:10.075
So one day, someone will have
their, like, head crushed,

00:05:10.075 --> 00:05:11.377
but their body
would be alright.

00:05:11.377 --> 00:05:13.613
I'll have my body crushed
or it will just fall apart.

00:05:13.613 --> 00:05:16.415
They'd keep me alive
in a jar or something.

00:05:16.415 --> 00:05:18.451
And then I'd pay a lot of money.
-Yeah.

00:05:18.451 --> 00:05:19.751
-Right?
I've got a bit of cash now.

00:05:19.751 --> 00:05:21.487
I'll go, "I'll have that body,"

00:05:21.487 --> 00:05:23.822
and then I'd put my head
on the new body.

00:05:23.822 --> 00:05:26.526
Statistically,
I will get a bigger penis.

00:05:26.526 --> 00:05:28.828
[ Laughter ]

00:05:28.828 --> 00:05:30.363
So...

00:05:32.331 --> 00:05:34.500
It won't be mine.
-Yeah.

00:05:36.101 --> 00:05:37.503
How long do you think --
Be honest.

00:05:37.503 --> 00:05:39.205
How long it would take --
-Sorry, sorry.

00:05:39.205 --> 00:05:41.240
[ Laughter ]

00:05:45.744 --> 00:05:47.746
-Let me tell you,
that's wishful thinking.

00:05:47.746 --> 00:05:51.550
I don't know who you think's
getting crushed by a bus.

00:05:51.550 --> 00:05:52.585
But how long
do you think it would take

00:05:52.585 --> 00:05:54.554
to get used to
a different penis?

00:05:56.922 --> 00:05:58.924
-I wouldn't be comfortable.
-Yeah.

00:05:58.924 --> 00:06:01.727
-Although,
it wouldn't be my hand either.

00:06:01.727 --> 00:06:02.795
-That's a good point.

00:06:02.795 --> 00:06:04.230
That would be the trickier part.

00:06:04.230 --> 00:06:06.365
-So, I'd just close my eyes.

00:06:06.365 --> 00:06:09.836
I'd just get
all the feelings, right?

00:06:09.836 --> 00:06:11.437
[ Laughter ]

00:06:13.005 --> 00:06:14.473
-So, I think
we've worked through that.

00:06:14.473 --> 00:06:16.142
I think if that --
Hopefully, if that's the last --

00:06:16.142 --> 00:06:18.043
If that's the last hurdle for
science, I hope they watch this.

00:06:18.043 --> 00:06:19.946
-Exactly, yeah.

00:06:21.447 --> 00:06:25.917
-You go out of your way to
explain irony in your special.

00:06:25.917 --> 00:06:29.355
I mean, I feel like the British
are very proud about

00:06:29.355 --> 00:06:32.291
the fact that they, I feel like,
have the best sense of irony.

00:06:32.291 --> 00:06:34.426
Did you feel the need to
explain irony for

00:06:34.426 --> 00:06:35.828
Americans specifically
or just comedy audiences?

00:06:35.828 --> 00:06:37.763
-No, no, no, no.
And I sort of do it as a joke.

00:06:37.763 --> 00:06:38.930
That is the joke
that I'm explaining,

00:06:38.930 --> 00:06:40.065
and I still get it wrong.

00:06:40.065 --> 00:06:42.468
So I explain -- I say --
I come out and I say

00:06:42.468 --> 00:06:46.438
something awful and then I say,
"That was irony.

00:06:46.438 --> 00:06:47.840
There's gonna be a bit of that
throughout the show.

00:06:47.840 --> 00:06:48.941
See if you can spot it."

00:06:48.941 --> 00:06:49.975
That's when I say something

00:06:49.975 --> 00:06:52.144
I don't really mean
for comic effect,

00:06:52.144 --> 00:06:54.580
and you, as an audience,
you laugh at the "wrong thing,"

00:06:54.580 --> 00:06:55.915
'cause you don't know
what the right thing is.

00:06:55.915 --> 00:06:58.050
So I sort of do it as
a joke to challenge them,

00:06:58.050 --> 00:07:00.052
and then I carry on
saying terrible things

00:07:00.052 --> 00:07:01.420
and hopefully they get it.

00:07:01.420 --> 00:07:04.122
-And it does seem fun,
because in a weird way, like --

00:07:04.122 --> 00:07:05.791
Not a weird way,
very on purpose.

00:07:05.791 --> 00:07:08.026
But you then involve them
in what the game is gonna be.

00:07:08.026 --> 00:07:09.361
-And I challenge them.

00:07:09.361 --> 00:07:10.663
-Yeah, and you keep catching
them off guard with it.

00:07:10.663 --> 00:07:12.465
-Exactly. And I've challenged
them to be offended

00:07:12.465 --> 00:07:13.866
or I told them it's a joke.

00:07:13.866 --> 00:07:17.002
And I think that's the big thing
now that people are, you know --

00:07:17.002 --> 00:07:18.403
It's always been the same.

00:07:18.403 --> 00:07:21.240
People have always been offended
by comedians or things they say,

00:07:21.240 --> 00:07:23.309
but we didn't used to
hear about it.

00:07:23.309 --> 00:07:25.110
You know,
we didn't used to have Twitter.

00:07:25.110 --> 00:07:27.613
To write a complaint
20 years ago, you'd go,

00:07:27.613 --> 00:07:30.682
"Dear BBC, I'm -- Oh, I can't
be bothered," you know?

00:07:30.682 --> 00:07:32.017
But now
someone tweets something,

00:07:32.017 --> 00:07:35.187
and the papers pick that up and
go, "People are furious.

00:07:35.187 --> 00:07:37.156
ZX143 said this."

00:07:37.156 --> 00:07:38.424
And it becomes a thing,
and it isn't.

00:07:38.424 --> 00:07:40.726
You should ignore it.
You should just ignore it.

00:07:40.726 --> 00:07:44.229
-I think that that is
very good advice,

00:07:44.229 --> 00:07:45.898
because I do think --
Especially when there's

00:07:45.898 --> 00:07:47.767
craft to the joke --
Like, for example,

00:07:47.767 --> 00:07:49.935
look at -- Because you do in
the beginning of the special.

00:07:49.935 --> 00:07:52.371
You set up like, "Here's the
game," to some degree,

00:07:52.371 --> 00:07:55.074
and each time,
there's never any laziness

00:07:55.074 --> 00:07:57.009
to the joke,
because the joke is always --

00:07:57.009 --> 00:07:58.177
There's a construction to it.

00:07:58.177 --> 00:08:01.046
-And I explain that,
you know, a joke isn't

00:08:01.046 --> 00:08:03.949
a window to the comedian's
true soul, you know?

00:08:03.949 --> 00:08:06.185
It's a joke.
I'll pretend to take a view.

00:08:06.185 --> 00:08:07.586
I'll pretend to be right wing.

00:08:07.586 --> 00:08:09.021
I'll pretend to be left wing.
I'll pretend to be clever.

00:08:09.021 --> 00:08:10.489
I'll pretend to be stupid.

00:08:10.489 --> 00:08:13.793
Whatever makes the joke -- You
know, it's all about the joke.

00:08:13.793 --> 00:08:16.295
And I think people
often get offended

00:08:16.295 --> 00:08:20.031
when they mistake the subject of
a joke with the actual target.

00:08:20.031 --> 00:08:22.635
And if you look at it, you know,
often, the target's me.

00:08:22.635 --> 00:08:25.838
It's my stupidity or me getting
something wrong or --

00:08:25.838 --> 00:08:29.107
And I think that that's
where it comes from,

00:08:29.107 --> 00:08:31.777
because -- And I deal in
taboo subjects on purpose,

00:08:31.777 --> 00:08:32.845
because I want to
take the audience

00:08:32.845 --> 00:08:34.547
to a place
it hasn't been before.

00:08:34.547 --> 00:08:36.182
But I also think
they're good to crunch.

00:08:36.182 --> 00:08:38.084
They're good --
There is a tension,

00:08:38.084 --> 00:08:39.484
and it's always okay in the end.

00:08:39.484 --> 00:08:40.886
And I think that's what --

00:08:40.886 --> 00:08:43.122
They're only taboo because
people want them to be taboo.

00:08:43.122 --> 00:08:44.824
People say,
"Oh, that's controversial"

00:08:44.824 --> 00:08:46.225
'cause they want you to shut up.

00:08:46.225 --> 00:08:49.194
That's why they say -- You know,
they want you to shut up.

00:08:49.194 --> 00:08:51.030
So don't shut up.

00:08:51.030 --> 00:08:52.531
-Your next special
is called "Armageddon."

00:08:52.531 --> 00:08:53.966
-Yes.

00:08:53.966 --> 00:08:56.435
-And, now, that seems very
in-line with the way you think.

00:08:56.435 --> 00:08:58.871
-Yeah. Yeah.

00:08:58.871 --> 00:09:00.539
-What are you
setting out to do here?

00:09:00.539 --> 00:09:04.143
-Again, it's me pondering how
the humanity will be wiped out.

00:09:04.143 --> 00:09:06.846
And I can't wait.

00:09:06.846 --> 00:09:08.214
-Yeah.

00:09:08.214 --> 00:09:10.749
-Do you think
you'll be here to see it?

00:09:10.749 --> 00:09:13.452
-I'll see some of it.

00:09:13.452 --> 00:09:15.220
I'll see the beginning of it.
-Yeah.

00:09:15.220 --> 00:09:19.425
-You know, but I think --
You know, I think young people

00:09:19.425 --> 00:09:21.227
now will have a very different
middle age to me.

00:09:21.227 --> 00:09:23.295
And it's my fault,
my generation,

00:09:23.295 --> 00:09:24.796
and the generation of
all the Boomers

00:09:24.796 --> 00:09:27.132
with the fossil fuels
and the global warming.

00:09:27.132 --> 00:09:29.301
So some of these 29 --
40 years' time,

00:09:29.301 --> 00:09:33.873
they will be in their apartments
with masks on, crying,

00:09:33.873 --> 00:09:36.107
like they do now over jokes.

00:09:36.107 --> 00:09:38.076
-Now, I will say this.

00:09:38.076 --> 00:09:39.211
I'm very happy to hear,
because we were

00:09:39.211 --> 00:09:41.246
talking about health,
and you are --

00:09:41.246 --> 00:09:42.248
Because you're not giving up.

00:09:42.248 --> 00:09:43.315
You're out. You're walking.

00:09:43.315 --> 00:09:45.150
You're very --
You try to be ambulatory

00:09:45.150 --> 00:09:46.585
a good amount of the time.

00:09:46.585 --> 00:09:50.555
-This is how you talk about
someone in a chair in a home.

00:09:50.555 --> 00:09:52.958
-Give a round --
Look, Ricky went for a walk.

00:09:52.958 --> 00:09:54.326
-Yeah.
[ Laughs ]

00:09:54.326 --> 00:09:56.962
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:09:56.962 --> 00:09:59.097
Listen, I don't care.

00:09:59.097 --> 00:10:02.534
I'll -- As I say,
as long as I can sort of

00:10:02.534 --> 00:10:05.171
still talk and drink, I don't
care if I end up in a bucket.

00:10:05.171 --> 00:10:06.371
-Yeah.

00:10:06.371 --> 00:10:07.873
-I'm just being
pushed around in a bucket of

00:10:07.873 --> 00:10:10.643
my own urine and feces
by a nurse,

00:10:10.643 --> 00:10:12.578
with a drip and that --

00:10:12.578 --> 00:10:14.046
-What if they say,
"We have bad news.

00:10:14.046 --> 00:10:17.449
We don't have a body for you.

00:10:17.449 --> 00:10:18.884
We have a lovely bucket."

00:10:18.884 --> 00:10:20.886
-That'd be fine.
-Just your head in a bucket.

00:10:20.886 --> 00:10:23.088
-And then, when it gets too
much, I'll just take the pill.

00:10:23.088 --> 00:10:25.023
Hopefully, that's what
I'm looking forward to.

00:10:25.023 --> 00:10:27.226
-I'll just go into CVS and go,
"Give me the pill,"

00:10:27.226 --> 00:10:28.527
and they go, "Are you sure?"

00:10:28.527 --> 00:10:31.163
I'll go, "Of course I'm sure.
[Bleep] look at me."

00:10:32.565 --> 00:10:33.666
-They go, "Here.

00:10:33.666 --> 00:10:36.736
Here's one
and here's one for free."

00:10:36.736 --> 00:10:38.504
Hey, thanks for being here.
Congrats again on the special.

00:10:38.504 --> 00:10:41.173
-Cheers. Thank you.
-Ricky Gervais.

00:10:41.173 --> 00:10:44.443
"Supernature" begins streaming
on Netflix May 24th.

